// queue.bs -- `switch`, the only branching construct (F7, ticket 17 §6).
//
// There is no `if`, no `else`, no `cond` and no ternary for this to sit beside.
// What makes that affordable is that a switch arm is the clause head's own
// pattern grammar, one level down -- so everything the head can dispatch on, an
// arm can dispatch on, and the exhaustiveness check is the same check.
//
//     bsc examples/Queue/queue.bs Decide false true false   -> :dead_letter
//     bsc examples/Queue/queue.bs Attempt 3                 -> :retried
//
// `Route` below compiles and is covered by a test, but CANNOT be run from this
// command line: `bs_run`'s argument reader has no spelling for a record, so
// `#{Kind => :'Queue.Message', ...}` is refused as unreadable. Measured, not
// assumed. It is the one function here you cannot watch work, which is a gap in
// the harness rather than in the language -- and worth naming, since `examples/`
// exists precisely so a capability is lookable-at.
//
// Every switch here is exhaustive with NO catch-all. That is the property worth
// looking at: a `_` would satisfy the compiler in every one of them, and none of
// them needs one.

module Queue

type Disposition = :ack | :dead_letter | :requeue
type Verdict     = :fresh | :retried | :exhausted

record Message { Id: int, Deliveries: int }

// THE TUPLE SUBJECT. A ladder of unrelated conditions takes a tuple, which is
// the clause head's own shape -- ticket 17 §6 made `switch` the only branching
// construct on the strength of exactly this. Prototypes 25a and 25c measured
// the readability cliff between four conditions and five; this is three.
//
// `true` and `false` are the language's two keyword atoms, so they are atoms
// here and not variables. That distinction is load-bearing: as identifiers they
// would match everything and the first arm would swallow the other three.
public Disposition Decide(bool ok, bool permanent, bool redelivered)
Decide(o, p, r) -> (o, p, r) switch {
    (true,  _,     _)     => :ack,
    (false, true,  _)     => :dead_letter,
    (false, false, false) => :requeue,
    (false, false, true)  => :requeue
}

// A GUARD ON AN ARM. The intervals are credited to the exhaustiveness check,
// which is why three arms cover `int` and the compiler agrees without a `_`.
//
// F2 has since landed the relational pattern, so these could be written
// `<= 1 => :fresh` and so on -- `wire.bs` does exactly that. They are left as
// guards deliberately: the two forms mean the same thing to the checker, and
// this file is what shows that a guard still credits. What is NOT built is the
// nested form C# spells `{ Deliveries: > 5 }` -- a relational pattern goes where
// a whole argument goes, and inside a record pattern you write the guard below.
public Verdict Attempt(int deliveries)
Attempt(n) -> n switch {
    m when m <= 1 => :fresh,
    m when m < 5  => :retried,
    m when m >= 5 => :exhausted
}

// The subject is any expression, not just a parameter -- which is ticket 17's
// answer to 01b's intermediate-value friction: you can branch on a projection
// without inventing a parameter to dispatch on.
public Disposition Route(Message m)
Route(m) -> m.Deliveries switch {
    n when n < 3  => :requeue,
    n when n >= 3 => :dead_letter
}
